WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.720
Phosphorus has no major gas, so no microbe can fix it from the air.

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.980
Nitrogen can be fixed by microbes.

3
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.940
So when a lake turns green with algae, which input should you cut, nitrogen or

4
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:17.940
phosphorus?

5
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:24.173
Both elements reach a lake as inputs, such as inflowing water, dust and fertiliser.

6
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:30.213
Only nitrogen has a second kind of input, fixation from the air by microbes.

7
00:00:31.613 --> 00:00:33.793
Which one sets how much algae a lake can hold?

8
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:40.313
David Schindler was invited to lead whole-lake experiments in northwestern Ontario.

9
00:00:41.693 --> 00:00:46.413
Small lakes were set aside, so whole ecosystems could be changed and watched for

10
00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:52.273
years. Short tests in bottles can miss slow changes, like a rise in nitrogen

11
00:00:52.273 --> 00:00:53.273
fixation.

12
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:58.187
Lake 226 was divided by a barrier into two basins.

13
00:00:59.587 --> 00:01:04.567
Both basins received nitrogen, as nitrate, and carbon, as the sugar sucrose.

14
00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:07.947
One basin also received phosphorus.

15
00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:10.387
The other got none.

16
00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:15.607
So the only difference in what each half was given was that one element.

17
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.380
Both halves had plenty of nitrogen and carbon to work with.

18
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:22.740
Only one had phosphorus.

19
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.200
Which half turns green?

20
00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:32.963
Only the phosphorus basin.

21
00:01:34.183 --> 00:01:38.263
It became highly eutrophic, rich in nutrients and dense with algae.

22
00:01:39.603 --> 00:01:41.443
The other basin stayed as it had been.

23
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:47.807
So in that lake, with nitrogen and carbon plentiful, phosphorus limited the algae.

24
00:01:49.087 --> 00:01:52.907
A nutrient is limiting when adding it on its own raises production.

25
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:59.260
Lake 227 was fertilised with both nitrogen and phosphorus for years.

26
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.500
Then the nitrogen fertiliser stopped.

27
00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.100
The phosphorus kept coming.

28
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:10.113
With its nitrogen input cut, you might expect the algae to fade.

29
00:02:11.493 --> 00:02:16.293
They did not. Cyanobacteria that fix nitrogen took over.

30
00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:22.153
They fixed nitrogen from the air, an input that kept coming after the fertiliser

31
00:02:22.153 --> 00:02:27.933
stopped. Its rate can still respond to phosphorus supply and other conditions.

32
00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:32.403
The lake stayed highly eutrophic, year after year.

33
00:02:33.743 --> 00:02:36.283
Algae stayed as abundant as the phosphorus allowed.

34
00:02:37.703 --> 00:02:39.843
Fixation had made up the missing nitrogen.

35
00:02:40.983 --> 00:02:45.063
These are small lakes in Ontario, and one lake is not a replicate.

36
00:02:46.503 --> 00:02:49.823
Other lakes, and many estuaries, can respond to nitrogen.

37
00:02:51.103 --> 00:02:56.003
And production is often co-limited, rising only when both nutrients are added

38
00:02:56.003 --> 00:02:57.003
together.

39
00:02:57.717 --> 00:03:02.237
So in these lakes, phosphorus supply set how much algae the lake could hold.

40
00:03:03.577 --> 00:03:05.517
Fixers can make up missing nitrogen.

41
00:03:06.817 --> 00:03:08.537
No microbe can fix phosphorus.
